---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import Layers from '../../components/Layers.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Schools & Traditions', href: '/explore/schools/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Schools and Traditions in Astrology"
  description="Hellenistic, medieval, modern psychological, evolutionary, and Vedic astrology, how the major approaches differ, and why knowing which one you're getting matters."
  path="/explore/schools/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Schools and traditions</h1>
        <p class="lede">Astrology is not one method. Knowing which tradition an astrologer works in tells you more about what your reading will be like than almost anything else.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.meetingWaves.src}
        srcset={ocean.meetingWaves.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.meetingWaves.alt}
        width={ocean.meetingWaves.width}
        height={ocean.meetingWaves.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Why this page exists</h2>
      <p>
        Most astrology writing online presents itself as simply "astrology," as though there were a
        single agreed body of technique. There isn't. Two competent astrologers can look at the same
        chart and produce different readings because they are using different systems: different
        house divisions, different planets, different assumptions about what a chart is for. That is
        worth understanding before you book anything.
      </p>

      <h2>Hellenistic astrology (roughly 1st century BCE – 7th century CE)</h2>
      <p>
        The origin of most Western technique, developed in Greco-Roman Egypt. Uses the seven visible
        planets, whole sign houses, <a href="/explore/dignities/">essential dignities</a>, sect
        (whether a chart is diurnal or nocturnal), and timing methods such as annual profections and
        zodiacal releasing. Largely lost to the Western world for centuries, and substantially
        recovered since the 1990s through translation projects, which is why it feels simultaneously
        ancient and new.
      </p>

      <h2>Medieval and Renaissance astrology</h2>
      <p>
        The Hellenistic material transmitted through Persian and Arabic astrologers, Māshāʾallāh,
        Abū Maʿshar, and others, then into Latin Europe. Highly technical, heavy on dignity, lots and
        elaborate prediction. This lineage produced <strong>horary astrology</strong>, which answers a
        specific question from a chart cast for the moment it is asked. I hold a Practitioner's Certificate
        in horary from the School of Traditional Astrology, which is why the classical material
        appears throughout this site.
      </p>

      <h2>Modern psychological astrology (20th century)</h2>
      <p>
        Emerging from Alan Leo's popularization and later Dane Rudhyar's humanistic approach and
        Jungian influence, this is the astrology most people have encountered. It shifted the
        question from "what will happen" to "who is this person becoming," incorporated the newly
        discovered outer planets, and largely set aside dignity and prediction in favour of character
        and growth.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.paperBoat} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Evolutionary astrology</h2>
      <p>
        A late-20th-century development placing Pluto and the lunar nodes at the centre, reading the
        chart as a soul's developmental trajectory. Explicitly incorporates reincarnation as a working
        premise, which some clients want and others emphatically do not, worth clarifying in advance.
      </p>

      <h2>Vedic (Jyotish) astrology</h2>
      <p>
        A separate and continuous Indian tradition, not a variant of the Western one. It uses the
        <em>sidereal</em> zodiac, anchored to the constellations rather than the seasons, so most
        planetary positions differ by roughly 24° from a Western chart, often placing planets in
        the previous sign. It has its own house logic, its own timing system (dashas), and its own
        extensive literature. A Vedic reading is a different discipline, not a second opinion.
      </p>

      <Layers kind="mo">
        <p>
          I describe myself as a modern astrologer with a classical lens, and that phrase is precise
          rather than decorative. I use traditional technique, dignity, sect, rulership, horary
          method, inside a modern conversation about your actual life and your actual choices. The
          old material gives structure and specificity; the modern frame keeps your agency at the
          centre where it belongs. <a href="/approach/">More on how I work.</a>
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <h2>What to ask an astrologer</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Which tradition do you primarily work in?</li>
        <li>Which house system do you use, and why?</li>
        <li>Do you use the modern outer planets, and do you use dignity?</li>
        <li>Is prediction part of your practice, or is your focus character and timing?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of these has a right answer. But an astrologer who can answer them clearly is one who
        knows what they're doing, and that is a genuinely useful screening question. More in
        <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-an-astrologer/">how to choose an astrologer</a>.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand heading="A classical lens, a modern conversation" body="Traditional technique, present-tense life, your choices at the centre." buttonLabel="Read about Mo's approach" buttonHref="/approach/" />
</BaseLayout>
